---
title: Next.js library
description: The GateKeeper Next.js BFF package — App Router route handlers, middleware refresh, and RSC-safe session reads.
section: Application libraries
---

`@gatekeeper/nextjs` is the Next.js (App Router) integration for GateKeeper. It is a
**backend-for-frontend (BFF)**, not a SPA: the Authorization Code + PKCE exchange runs on the
server, and tokens live only inside an **AES-256-GCM encrypted, `httpOnly` cookie**. The browser
never sees an access or refresh token.

It binds Next.js route/middleware idioms to the framework-agnostic `@gatekeeper/bff-core`. The
package source lives at `packages/gatekeeper-nextjs`; a runnable example is at `frontends/nextjs`.

## Confidential client — client secret required

Unlike the SPA packages, a BFF keeps a real **client secret** on the server. Register a
**confidential** client and keep the secret in server-only env (never `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`). Because
calls are server→server, **no CORS entry is needed**.

## Configure the client

```ts
// lib/gatekeeper.ts — server-only
import { createNextGateKeeper } from '@gatekeeper/nextjs';

export const gatekeeper = createNextGateKeeper({
	idpBaseUrl: process.env.IDP_BASE_URL!,
	authUiBaseUrl: process.env.AUTH_UI_BASE_URL!,
	tenantId: process.env.OAUTH_TENANT_ID!,
	clientId: process.env.OAUTH_CLIENT_ID!,
	clientSecret: process.env.OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET,
	redirectUri: process.env.OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI!, // …/auth/callback
	scope: process.env.OAUTH_SCOPE ?? 'openid profile email',
	sessionSecret: process.env.SESSION_SECRET!, // openssl rand -hex 32
	secureCookies: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production'
});
```

`SESSION_SECRET` protects the encrypted session cookie; rotating it invalidates existing sessions.

## Wire the route handlers

Each auth endpoint is one App Router route handler:

```ts
// app/auth/sign-in/route.ts
export const GET = gatekeeper.signInHandler;

// app/auth/callback/route.ts
export const GET = gatekeeper.callbackHandler;

// app/auth/sign-out/route.ts
export const POST = gatekeeper.signOutHandler;
```

## Silent refresh in middleware

```ts
// middleware.ts
import { gatekeeper } from './lib/gatekeeper';

export const middleware = gatekeeper.middleware;
export const config = { matcher: ['/((?!_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico).*)'] };
```

`middleware()` reads the session cookie, refreshes the access token when it is near expiry
(rotation coalesced so concurrent requests don't reuse a refresh token), and writes the rotated
cookie onto the response.

## Read the session in server components

```tsx
// app/page.tsx (server component)
import { gatekeeper } from '@/lib/gatekeeper';

export default async function Home() {
	const session = await gatekeeper.getSession();
	const user = await gatekeeper.getUser();
	return <p>{session ? `Signed in as ${user?.email}` : 'Signed out'}</p>;
}
```

`getSession()`/`getUser()` are RSC-safe **read-only** — they never mutate cookies (refresh happens
in middleware). Guard a page by redirecting when there is no session:

```tsx
import { redirect } from 'next/navigation';
const session = await gatekeeper.getSession();
if (!session) redirect('/auth/sign-in');
```

## The flow

1. **Sign in** — `GET /auth/sign-in` mints PKCE + `state` + `nonce`, parks them in short-lived
   `httpOnly` cookies, and redirects to the hosted login.
2. **Callback** — `GET /auth/callback` validates `state`, exchanges the code (PKCE + secret),
   verifies the id_token `nonce`, and seals the `gk_session` cookie.
3. **Silent refresh** — middleware rotates the token before expiry.
4. **Sign out** — `POST /auth/sign-out` clears the session cookie.

## Example app

`frontends/nextjs` runs on dev port 5179. Register a confidential client with
`frontends/nextjs/scripts/register-client.sh`, paste `OAUTH_CLIENT_ID`/`OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET` into
`.env`, set `SESSION_SECRET`, then `npm install && npm run dev`.
